From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iasnezhkov <iasnezhkov@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 04 Oct 2026 05:42:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] perfmgr: retry partially failed node transitions

Required node errors already preserve the previous selected index and
request a retry in 500ms or sooner. A failed transition may nevertheless
write an earlier path, accept a prefix before a later error, truncate a
file, or replace a held descriptor. Cancellation or expiry back to the
previous index then skips the write despite these side effects.

Require a rewrite after a required failure, including when the latest
selection equals the previous index. Clear the existing reset state only
on the already-existing successful update path. Preserve all values,
hint durations, request priority, defaults, retry cadence and intentional
AllowFailure/ENOENT behavior. Add no field or symbol.

The mutable reset-state getter already becomes false on successful
updates. Its consumers in the exact Google/Lineage PowerHAL subtrees are
only initialization/property tests. Unknown outside clients are not
claimed absent. Current configured File nodes are single-path and their
stores have no source-proved short successful write; this is generic
restoration hardening, not demonstrated current-device energy savings.

Source-bound ASan/UBSan controls exercise partial/default/prior-index
restoration, descriptor lifetime, actual two-pass looper scheduling,
expiry, latest selection and optional absence. Separate exact native
android-base write-loop controls prove prefix acceptance before EIO.
The 500ms retry sleep does not bound blocking syscalls, mutex waits,
zero-progress writes, external ownership or hardware application.
---
diff --git a/power-libperfmgr/libperfmgr/FileNode.cc b/power-libperfmgr/libperfmgr/FileNode.cc
--- a/power-libperfmgr/libperfmgr/FileNode.cc
+++ b/power-libperfmgr/libperfmgr/FileNode.cc
@@ -87,6 +87,7 @@
                     // Retry in 500ms or sooner
                     expire_time = std::min(expire_time, std::chrono::milliseconds(500));
                     successfullyUpdated = false;
+                    reset_on_init_ = true;
                 }
             } else {
                 // For regular file system, we need fsync
